<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Helicon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Helicon is a mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, rising to about 1,750 metres between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-copais">Lake Copais</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. In Greek myth it was sacred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>, whose sanctuary in the Valley of the Muses held festival contests, and the spring Hippocrene was said to have been struck from it by the hoof of Pegasus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> tells that the Muses gave him his poetic gift there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-helicon.jpg" alt="Mount Helicon" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Skene of Rubislaw · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Helicon,_Parnassus_and_Plain_of_Thebes_-_Skene_James_-_1838-1845.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
